---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="About" description="Learn about CheckYourCalendar, its mission to bridge utility and cultural storytelling, and the science behind calendar conversions.">
  <Header activeTab="about" />

  <main class="container main-content">
    <article class="editorial-article">
      <header class="article-header">
        <h1 class="article-title">About CheckYourCalendar</h1>
        <p class="article-subtitle">Bridging mathematical coordinates and cultural stories</p>
      </header>

      <section class="article-body">
        <p class="lead">
          Time is not merely a number. Every day has a context. Every date has a history. Yet, timekeeping sites are traditionally split between dry utility grids and hyper-siloed religious portals. CheckYourCalendar exists to bridge that gap.
        </p>

        <h2>Our Mission: The Date Discovery Path</h2>
        <p>
          We operate under a simple, progressive information architecture:
        </p>
        <div class="mission-path">
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">1</span>
            <strong>Date</strong>
            <p>A specific coordinate in time</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-arrow"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">arrow_forward</span></div>
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">2</span>
            <strong>Calendar</strong>
            <p>How different cultures map that date</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-arrow"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">arrow_forward</span></div>
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">3</span>
            <strong>Observance</strong>
            <p>What festivals or fasts occur today</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-arrow"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">arrow_forward</span></div>
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">4</span>
            <strong>Meaning & Context</strong>
            <p>The cultural and historical significance behind it</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          CheckYourCalendar is designed to let you check a conversion instantly, but walk away with a deeper understanding of what today actually represents across human traditions.
        </p>

        <h2 id="methodology">Calculation Methodology</h2>
        <p>
          Calendar calculations are complex and high-trust. We utilize standard, open-source astronomical and mathematical adapters to compute dates programmatically:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Solar Calendars</strong> (e.g., Gregorian, Persian) use precise solar cycle calculations, mapping astronomical coordinates or leap rules directly.</li>
          <li><strong>Lunisolar Calendars</strong> (e.g., Hebrew, Chinese) balance the lunar cycles with solar correction. We use official astronomical models to calculate leap additions and seasonal adjustments.</li>
          <li><strong>Pure Lunar Calendars</strong> (e.g., Hijri) follow the moon sighting cycle. For calculation predictability, we utilize the Umm al-Qura calendar civil calculations as a standard, while clearly documenting that visual sighting variances may differ by 1 day regionally.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Research & Verification Standards</h2>
        <p>
          We hold cultural and religious content to high accuracy standards:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No Mass AI Filler:</strong> All descriptions and historical contexts are curated and fact-checked. We do not mass-generate spiritual claims or legends.</li>
          <li><strong>Attribution:</strong> We reference academic publications, astronomical ephemerides, government gazettes, and established cultural/religious archives.</li>
          <li><strong>Neutral Tone:</strong> We present cultural and religious traditions informatively and respectfully, without promotion or sectarian bias.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>How do I check and compare today's date across multiple calendar systems?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                When you open CheckYourCalendar, the homepage automatically detects your local date and displays its conversion across all supported calendar systems simultaneously. Alongside today’s date in solar, lunar, and lunisolar traditions, the page shows any major cultural or religious observances occurring today. You can also select any date using the calendar picker to explore its coordinates across world traditions.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>Which calendar systems are supported on CheckYourCalendar?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                CheckYourCalendar currently supports date conversions and cultural contexts for 8 major historical, religious, and civil calendar systems alongside the standard Gregorian calendar: Vikram Samvat (Gujarati/Hindu), Islamic Hijri, Hebrew, Persian (Solar Hijri), Indian National (Saka), Chinese Traditional (Lunisolar), Coptic, and Ethiopian. Additional calendar systems may be added over time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>How does calendar conversion work between different timekeeping systems?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Every calendar system measures time from a specific historical starting point (its epoch) and follows distinct astronomical or civil rules. Calendar conversion works by translating a date into a standardized universal time coordinate and then calculating the corresponding year, month, day, and cycle in the target calendar using verified astronomical formulas and calendar algorithms.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>What is the difference between solar, lunar, and lunisolar calendar systems?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Calendar systems are categorized by the celestial cycles they track:</p>
              <p>
                Solar calendars, such as Gregorian, Persian, and Indian Saka, track the Earth's orbit around the Sun (about 365.24 days) to keep dates aligned with the solar seasons.
              </p>
              <p>
                Pure lunar calendars, such as the Islamic Hijri calendar, follow the 12 lunar phase cycles (about 354 days), causing dates to move through all four seasons over a roughly 33-year cycle.
              </p>
              <p>
                Lunisolar calendars, such as Hindu Vikram Samvat, Hebrew, and Chinese calendars, track lunar months while periodically adding a leap month to stay synchronized with the solar year.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>Why do different calendars have different years and dates for the same day?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Different cultures anchored their calendars to different historical, astronomical, or spiritual milestones as their starting year (epoch). For example, the Gregorian calendar uses the Common Era (CE), while Vikram Samvat, the Islamic Hijri calendar, and the Hebrew calendar use different historical eras and starting points. Because systems also use different month lengths and leap-year rules, the numerical date for the same physical day naturally differs across calendars.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>Why do traditional festival and holiday dates change every year on the Gregorian calendar?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                Many traditional cultural and religious holidays—such as Diwali, Eid, Passover, and Lunar New Year—are scheduled according to lunar or lunisolar calendars rather than the solar Gregorian calendar. Because a lunar year of 12 moon cycles is about 10 to 11 days shorter than a 365-day solar year, these festivals can move relative to the Gregorian calendar from year to year, while lunisolar calendars use leap months to maintain longer-term alignment with the solar year.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>Why might a calendar date or traditional observance differ by one day depending on location?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                In many traditional timekeeping systems, dates and festival timings depend on local astronomical events—such as when the sun rises in a specific city or when the new crescent moon is physically sighted in a given region. Timezone differences can also place the same astronomical event on different local civil dates. To provide consistent conversions, CheckYourCalendar uses standardized astronomical baselines and documented reference standards.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>How does CheckYourCalendar ensure accuracy in its calendar calculations?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                CheckYourCalendar uses established astronomical algorithms, open-source calculation libraries, and recognized civil standards, such as the Umm al-Qura standard for Islamic dates and standard solar-lunar calculation models for Indian Panchang. These methods are designed to provide consistent calendar calculations, but some religious or regional observances may follow local authorities, moon sightings, or regional customs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>What is the difference between a calendar date and an observance?</span>
              <span class="material-symbols-outlined faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>
                A calendar date is a mathematical coordinate in time within a specific system, such as a given day, month, and year. An observance is a cultural festival, religious holy day, fasting period, national holiday, or international commemoration that takes place on or spans across designated calendar coordinates. A single calendar date can host multiple observances across different global traditions.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .main-content {
    padding-top: var(--spacing-xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .editorial-article {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .article-header {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-lg);
  }

  .article-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  .article-subtitle {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-variant);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .article-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-on-background);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.625rem;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-outline-variant);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .article-body p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-on-background);
  }

  .article-body ul {
    list-style-type: square;
    padding-left: var(--spacing-lg);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-on-background);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  /* FAQ Accordion Section Styling */
  .faq-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-sm);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .faq-item {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-outline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: border-color var(--transition-fast), background-color var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .faq-item[open] {
    background-color: var(--color-surface-dim);
    border-color: var(--color-outline-variant);
  }

  .faq-question {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    padding: var(--spacing-md);
    cursor: pointer;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    list-style: none;
    user-select: none;
    gap: var(--spacing-sm);
    transition: color var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .faq-question::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .faq-icon {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    transition: transform var(--transition-fast);
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .faq-item[open] .faq-icon {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  .faq-question:hover {
    color: var(--color-primary-container);
  }

  .faq-question:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
    outline-offset: -2px;
  }

  .faq-answer {
    padding: 0 var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .faq-answer p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-variant);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .faq-answer a {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* Mission Path Bento style timeline */
  .mission-path {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-sm);
    background-color: var(--color-surface-dim);
    padding: var(--spacing-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    margin: var(--spacing-sm) 0;
  }

  .path-step {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-outline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: var(--spacing-md);
    position: relative;
  }

  .step-num {
    position: absolute;
    top: var(--spacing-xs);
    right: var(--spacing-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-primary-container);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .path-step strong {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--color-primary);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }

  .path-step p {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-variant);
    margin-top: 4px;
  }

  .path-arrow {
    display: none;
    justify-content: center;
    color: var(--color-outline-variant);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .mission-path {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
    }

    .path-step {
      width: 22%;
      min-height: 120px;
    }

    .path-arrow {
      display: flex;
    }
  }
</style>
